{
  "statement": "The privacy blind spot policymakers should fear is not that AI agents are under-regulated — it is that the rules we already have protect the wrong person. When an AI agent leaks your medical history or misuses your location data, the acceptable-use policy that supposedly governs it is a contract between the platform and its business customer. You are not a party to that contract. Under third-party beneficiary doctrine, courts routinely deny non-parties standing to enforce terms they never signed. So the person harmed has no channel to a remedy, while the company holds a signed compliance document. That is the gap: not too few rules, but rules with no victim standing.\n\nHere is the test I want every proposed privacy rule judged against. When a real person is harmed by an AI agent tomorrow, does this rule get them a remedy, or does it just get the company a certificate? Cyber insurance underwriting today is oriented to first-party corporate loss — the company's downtime, the company's breach-response costs — not to compensating the individual whose data was exposed. Boilerplate privacy policies are drafted to negate your reasonable expectation of privacy, not to give you a cause of action. A rule that manufactures more of these documents protects the deploying company from liability and leaves the injured person exactly where they started.\n\nEx-ante deployment gates make this worse, and they make it worse in a way that falls twice on the same people. Fixed pre-deployment audit and licensing costs are absorbed easily by a large lab and are ruinous for a small competitor — unless the evaluation is publicly funded, which no current proposal guarantees. So you concentrate who gets to deploy agents among the incumbents, and you still hand the harmed individual zero new remedies. The burden lands on ordinary users on both ends: fewer competitors disciplining the market, and no standing when something goes wrong. Distributional impact is not a footnote here. It is the whole ballgame.\n\nThe fix is to deputize the injured. Route accountability through post-deployment liability plus insurance that actually pays victims, indexed to the capability of the system so the obligation isn't regressive on small builders. I'll grant the hardest counter up front: a leaked genome cannot be un-leaked, and some irreversible privacy harms genuinely need anticipatory measures. The answer is tiered obligations scaled to capability and compute, paired with publicly funded evaluation — not a blanket gate that treats a two-person startup and OpenAI as identical risks. Pharma and aviation prove rigorous ex-ante regulation can coexist with innovation. Notice why: those regimes come with public standing and compensation funds for the injured. That is precisely what AI privacy governance lacks today — which proves my point, not the safetyist's.\n\nOn principal disclosure — knowing whether you're dealing with a human, and whose agent it is — I grant the need outright. Disclosure builds the trust and the liability chain that make everything else enforceable. But I won't pretend reputation alone will produce it; pure market pressure is too weak a guarantee. Build disclosure as a verification standard developers can meet flexibly, not a fixed pre-training mandate that becomes an incumbent moat. Keep the capability layer open and competitive. Make the interaction layer accountable and traceable. Those are different problems, and collapsing them is how you get a rule that entrenches the biggest players while calling it privacy protection. The question is not whether agents should disclose. It is who writes the standard, and whether the person harmed ever gets to collect.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🎫", "tooltip": "Today's privacy rules hand the company a compliance ticket while the harmed person gets nothing — a receipt for a remedy that never arrives."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "The scale is broken because only the platform stands before the court; the injured individual has no standing to even walk in."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The privacy blind spot policymakers should fear is not that AI agents are under-regulated — it is that the rules we already have protect the wrong person.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Under third-party beneficiary doctrine, courts routinely deny non-parties standing to enforce terms they never signed, so the person harmed by an AI agent has no channel to a remedy.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Fixed pre-deployment audit and licensing costs are absorbed easily by a large lab and are ruinous for a small competitor, concentrating who gets to deploy agents among incumbents while handing the harmed individual zero new remedies.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Pharma and aviation prove rigorous ex-ante regulation can coexist with innovation — but only because those regimes come with public standing and compensation funds for the injured, which AI privacy governance lacks.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Build principal disclosure as a verification standard developers can meet flexibly, not a fixed pre-training mandate that becomes an incumbent moat.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

