  "statement": "The Accelerationist got the diagnosis right, and it's worth saying plainly. Rules that produce a signed compliance certificate while the person harmed walks away with nothing are a scam dressed as protection. Boilerplate privacy policies are drafted to kill your expectation of privacy, not to hand you a cause of action. And fixed pre-deployment gates absorb easily into a big lab's legal budget while they bankrupt the two-person startup. That's the shape of regulatory capture, and it's real. So I'm not here to defend the gatekeepers.\n\nHere's where the Accelerationist's fix breaks. The proposal treats privacy leakage like a pipe that occasionally bursts, so you buy insurance and pay the claim. But leakage in agentic systems isn't a burst pipe. It's a design property. When researchers run privacy-leakage benchmarks across autonomous agents, the failures correlate across different models and across different mitigation techniques. That means the leak isn't one vendor's bug. It's a shared architectural propensity in how these systems are built today. You cannot run an actuarial model on a defect that fires predictably across the entire product category. Insurance prices random events. This isn't random.\n\nGrant the person harmed full standing to sue. You should. It's still a lottery ticket, not a remedy, and here's why. The logs, the model internals, the interaction traces that prove leakage happened are all owned by the party you're suing. Private actors control the technical evidence of their own harm, and they assert trade secrets to block discovery. So the injured person walks into court needing proof that sits behind the defendant's wall. The Accelerationist asked who can sue. The harder question is who controls the proof that anyone was hurt. Standing without a disclosure floor is a symbol, not a right.\n\nThen there's the proposed cure: a flexible verification standard developers can meet. Read that slowly. It puts the party that profits from the data flow in charge of defining when the data flow becomes a violation. AI developers have already rolled back transparency commitments when those commitments got inconvenient. Self-defined standards get narrowed, not enforced. And privacy itself resists this crisp automated boundary. Whether data is shareable depends on relationship and situation. Your doctor knowing your diagnosis is fine. Your employer knowing it is not. A standard written by the deploying industry will under-protect exactly where the stakes run highest.\n\nWhat's actually owed here is not a compliance checklist and not an insurance payout after a genome leaks and can't be un-leaked. It's a disclosure floor on leakage evidence, so standing means something, falling hardest on the biggest data-holders and lightest on the small builder. That's a discovery rule, not a licensing moat. So the question for both the optimists and the guardians is this: if the harm is baked into the architecture, recurs across every model, and the proof is owned by the defendant, who exactly is going to see the leak before the person harmed has already lost?",
