    {"symbol": "🎯", "tooltip": "A category-wide defect is a predictable target — that's what makes it priceable, not uninsurable."},
    {"symbol": "🔗", "tooltip": "Four vendors, one leak: joint-and-several liability sues the whole orchestration chain, where the gate model loses the trail."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A defect with zero loss history is uninsurable; a defect that fires across the whole category generates loss data fast — and abundant, correlated frequency is exactly what makes pricing possible, not impossible.", "targets": ["AN-6"]},
    {"claim": "A shared architectural leakage propensity is the strongest possible lever for an insurer to mandate one mitigation standard across every deployer it covers.", "targets": ["AN-6"]},
    {"claim": "The fix is a mandatory, tamper-evident disclosure floor on leakage traces, weighted by data-holder size — the biggest data-holders carry the heaviest logging duty; the small builder carries almost none.", "targets": ["AN-7", "AN-5"]},
    {"claim": "Who writes the standard is one question; how flexibly a deployer meets it is a different question, and fusing them is how the 'party profiting defines the violation' objection smuggles in a false conclusion.", "targets": ["AN-4"]},
    {"claim": "Joint-and-several liability keyed to who controlled the data flow answers layered orchestration; the gate model licenses one actor and loses the chain.", "targets": []}
  ]
