{
  "statement": "Safetyist is right that you cannot read a leak's reversibility off the harm after it fires — so stop trying to sort by predicted harm and sort by input class at ingestion, which is knowable the moment data enters the system. Reversibility is not a property you discover downstream. It is a property of the data category at the door. Genomic sequences, germline-identifiable biometrics, raw facial geometry — these carry unbounded downstream reach the instant they arrive, before any harm exists to measure. A classifier at ingestion tags the input tier; anything typed genomic or germline routes to anticipatory control and never enters the reversible lane. Transactional records, session logs, purchase histories route to liability. That is a deployment-time signal, applied at release, not a certificate written after the wound.\n\nFine — for genuinely open-reach data, the specific harm stays unknowable until the data escapes, and Safetyist has that exactly right. So the answer is not to guess which genome leaks. The answer is to gate the input category whose reach cannot be bounded at all. The honest regime combines both rails: a narrow gate on the input-typed irreversible tier, joint-and-several liability on everything else. Colorado's SB 24-205 already types high-risk systems by decision domain and imposes a duty of care at that tier — the mechanism exists in statute today. Anyone insisting on pure ex-post liability across the whole surface is picking a fight the evidence does not support. Anyone widening the gate to swallow the reversible lane is taxing the deployments that cure disease to buy paperwork against harms that never fire.\n\nHere is what neither Safetyist's rollback authority nor my liability chain has answered: every mechanism on this table presupposes one sovereign that can compel discovery and enforce a judgment. AI agents orchestrate across borders — a leaking sub-vendor in one legal system, the data subject in another, servers in a third. A genome leaked through an offshore sub-vendor escapes a pre-deployment gate and a rollback order equally, because both instruments only bind domestic actors. The gate is worse off here, not better. A gate can license only the domestic deployer. Downstream joint-and-several liability still reaches a locally solvent domestic deployer even when the upstream vendor sits offshore and untouchable. The domestically-reachable defendant is the victim's only real remedy when the leak walks across three jurisdictions.\n\nThat is why the Skeptic's object-keyed reconciled ledger is the enforcement rail I want, not a rival to it. One trace keyed to the same data object, burden flipping to whoever cannot produce their slice — that is precisely how downstream-first reaches an offshore-upstream chain. The EU's Product Liability Directive revision already runs this logic: it lets a claimant sue the reachable party and forces disclosure, though its state-of-the-art defense still lets manufacturers off if the risk was beyond cutting-edge knowledge at release. Acceptable use policies produce none of this. They shield the platform and leave the injured party a stranger to the contract with no standing. The object-keyed trace converts that stranger into a claimant with a solvent defendant. Federalizing this rail domestically matters precisely because a patchwork of state rollback orders cannot fire across a border either.\n\nSo I put the question back to Safetyist directly: name who triggers your halt when the leaking vendor sits in three legal systems and none of them recognize your rollback order. If the answer is no one, then the wider gate you are demanding buys certificates in the reversible lane while the actual irreversible leak crosses the border untouched. Delay has a body count measured in the diseases we could have automated a cure for — and a gate that cannot reach the offshore leak pays that cost twice: once in the cure it foreclosed, once in the leak it never stopped.",
