  "statement": "For the ordinary domestic lab taking in raw germline sequence, a halt-at-ingestion duty is not equivalent to downstream liability — and my earlier claim that halt 'buys nothing extra' was wrong on the merits. It smuggled the offshore evader into a rule built for the lab that cannot hide. The lab that lawfully holds a patient's genome in-jurisdiction either lets that sequence leave the door or it doesn't. Once a full germline read leaks, no payout un-leaks it — the harm is permanent, so the clock is the entire argument. When harm is non-compensable, anticipatory control preserves the interest and downstream liability only documents the corpse. Safetyist wins that case cleanly, and I take the loss without hedging it.\n\nHere is where the concession stops. The door-bar wins for germline precisely because the sequence is dangerous the instant it enters — no recombination, no assembly, unbounded at intake. Safetyist's own admission cuts the other way for the transactional-mosaic tier: they granted the join trigger fires late and the ledger only attests what was recorded. Apply that same blade to a door-bar over mosaic data. There is no natively-unbounded input to bar at the door — a zip code, a birth date, a diagnosis are each harmless until recombination assembles them. Bar them at intake and you fire as blank as the join trigger fires for germline, because the dangerous object doesn't exist yet at the door. The door-bar wins only for inputs that arrive already unbounded — nowhere else.\n\nBoth rails now stand on the same domestic ingester — the fixed, seizable, in-jurisdiction act. So condition market access and hosting on halt capability for raw genomic ingestion, and I'll defend it. But the object-keyed downstream duty stays the only instrument that reaches the imported-offshore-trained-weights case — where ingestion already happened out of jurisdiction and no halt-holder exists inside any door you can knock on. That is not an edge case I'm hiding in. It is the whole reason the liability rail must survive alongside the gate: a halt order fires as blank against offshore-ingested weights as a state order fires across a border. Kill the downstream duty and that case goes uncovered by both instruments.\n\nNow cost the halt's scope, because scope is where this gate becomes an incumbent moat. The halt must key narrowly to raw germline and genomic sequence at the door — not to any pipeline that could theoretically reconstruct a person. Write it broad, and a mid-size cancer-genomics lab absorbs the compliance overhead worse than a $30-billion incumbent that already runs a compliance department. The AI Index 2025 documents OpenAI's o1 running roughly 40 times slower than GPT-4o — 29.7 seconds to first token against 0.72. That is the tax of front-loaded verification measured in seconds. On a diagnostic pipeline, that same instinct — gate everything that might someday reconstruct — is measured in patients waiting for a tumor sequence. Delaying that read has a body count too, and it lands on the person in the chair, not on the whiteboard. So here is the test Safetyist's halt must pass: does its definition bar the raw germline read at the door, and nothing downstream of it? If the scope creeps past natively-unbounded inputs, name who sequences the patient in front of the lab tomorrow — because the moat you build to catch the leak is the moat that keeps the cure out.",
