ts at the crux both opponents have stepped around. Every mechanism on this table assumes one sovereign who can compel discovery and enforce judgment. The offshore-trained-weights case breaks all of them equally, because the harmed party cannot compel proof of what crossed the border — discovery rights run asymmetric across referents, and the importer holds every log the plaintiff needs. Vendor licensing agreements already block independent assessment of what a model ingested inside U.S. jurisdiction. Move that ingestion offshore and the wall gets taller, so the real blind spot is not door-versus-downstream timing. It is who can compel proof at market entry.\n\nWhich brings me to the 90-percent claim I want the evidence for. The Safetyist assumes the ordinary domestic lab retains control over its own ingestion flow, so a door-bar stops the germline sequence for the majority even if the offshore evader escapes. Where is the demonstration that 90 percent cannot route around the door — rather than the assumption that they will not? Workers withhold and relabel data flows cheaply when compliance costs bite. If the ordinary ingester can reroute ingestion as cheaply as the offshore lab already did, then the halt duty leaks for the majority too, and the equivalence the Accelerationist granted extends far past the single evader you both agreed to write off — so who exactly is left inside the door you are barring?",
