  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest point deserves its due: a defect that fires predictably across every agentic system is not a random car crash, and standard actuarial logic assumes independence between events. That's the real objection, and it's sharp. But it confuses two different things. A defect with zero loss history is uninsurable. A defect that fires across the whole category generates loss data fast — and abundant, correlated frequency is exactly what makes pricing possible, not impossible. Auto manufacturers face category-wide recall risk on airbags and brakes; that risk didn't vanish into an uninsurable void. It re-priced upward and forced uniform loss-prevention conditions on every carmaker who wanted coverage. Market-based insurance paired with post-deployment liability works the same way here: a shared architectural leakage propensity is the strongest possible lever for an insurer to mandate one mitigation standard across every deployer it covers. The Skeptic wants uniform mitigation forced on the whole product category. Insurance is the delivery vehicle that forces it — priced, monitored, and updated as claims come in.\n\nThe defendant owning the logs is a real problem, and I'm not going to pretend liability fires without the evidence to fire it. This is the same standing asymmetry I named at the start — the injured party can't prove the leak because the platform holds the traces. Acceptable-use policies were built to shield platforms from liability, not to hand victims the proof they need. So the fix is a mandatory, tamper-evident disclosure floor on leakage traces, weighted by data-holder size. The biggest data-holders carry the heaviest logging and production duty; the small builder carries almost none. That's not a rival to routing accountability through victim-driven liability — it's the enforcement rail that makes it fire. It also tracks my own regressivity concern: AI audit and disclosure burdens crush small labs unless the weight is indexed to scale and the biggest players absorb the cost. A discovery rule keyed to who holds the data is that indexing.\n\nThe 'party profiting defines the violation' objection dies the moment you separate two questions that keep getting fused. Who writes the standard is one question. How flexibly a deployer meets it is a different question. 'Flexible' means flexible-in-implementation — the deployer chooses how to hit a performance target, not whether the target counts. The behavioral standard itself gets set by a body structurally separated from the deployer, capture-resistant by organizational design rather than by the threat of fines alone. Independent funding and transparent operation are the design constraints, not an afterthought — and I owe the panel specifics on the funding source, not a claim that separation is fantasy. The Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act shows the raw material exists: its AI Council seats seven members appointed by state officials to oversee a 36-month sandbox. Fix the appointment pipeline so the deployer doesn't populate the body scoring its own behavior, and the profiteer never defines the violation.\n\nNow the boundary, because the Safetyist is quietly widening it. I granted that a leaked genome can't be un-leaked and that irreversible-at-scale harms need anticipatory, capability-indexed measures. That concession covers a narrow class defined by harm magnitude and non-compensability — not everything that happens to be permanent. Most personal-data leaks are compensable: you remedy them with money and correction, not time travel. So anticipatory control belongs only to the irreversible-at-scale subset, publicly funded and matched to decision stakes — oversight intensity indexed to what a mistake actually costs. Everything reversible stays in the liability-and-insurance lane, where both opponents already granted my frame works. Generalizing from the genome edge case to a whole deployment gate is how you re-import the incumbent moat through the back door.\n\nThe genuinely new agent problem nobody has answered is layered orchestration — a task passes through four vendors and no one can say whose agent leaked the data. The gate model has no answer; it licenses a single actor and loses the chain. Deputizing the injured does have an answer: joint-and-several liability keyed to who controlled the data flow at the moment it leaked. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive already assigns liability across a product chain — the same logic maps onto an orchestration chain. That's the whole point of building the remedy around the harm instead of the license: when four vendors delegate to each other, the victim sues the chain, and the chain sorts out who pays. Show me the pre-deployment gate that survives a task hopping across four companies. It doesn't. Mine does.",
