{
  "statement": "The tamper-evident disclosure floor is real reinforcement, and it strengthens exactly the lane where it belongs — the reversible one. Logging-by-default is infrastructure I demanded first; a system that documents who touched the data flow lets liability fire where a leak can be corrected, a payment made, a record scrubbed. The disclosure floor is a receipt. A receipt proves the transaction happened. A receipt is not a rollback. For a genome the Accelerationist already conceded cannot be un-leaked, the most tamper-evident log in the world arrives one moment too late — it certifies the harm at the instant the harm becomes permanent. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive, cited by the Accelerationist, still carries a state-of-the-art defense that exempts manufacturers when they prove the science was cutting-edge at release — meaning even a perfect log routes to a compensation fight the plaintiff can lose, over data that is already gone.\n\nThe narrow subset the Accelerationist wants to carve — irreversible-at-scale, defined by magnitude and non-compensability — assumes reversibility is a property you can read off a harm at deployment. It is not. You learn whether a leak was compensable only after the data has escaped and you watch what happens next. That is the trap. The Accelerationist proposes to sort leaks into the liability lane by a label — reversible — that cannot be reliably applied until the leak has already fired. You cannot triage into an ex-post remedy a harm whose non-compensability is unknowable ex ante. The subset where you must decide before you can know is not a genome edge case. It is every leak whose downstream reach you cannot bound at the moment of release — which is most of them, once inference and re-identification compound over years.\n\nThe insurance delivery vehicle depends on one load-bearing assumption: that a correlated defect re-prices rather than becomes uninsurable. Airbag recalls stagger across time and models — Takata's ran from 2013 through 2020, diversifiable across a fleet, which is why insurers absorbed them. A shared architectural leakage propensity does the opposite. It fires simultaneously across every deployer running the same model, on the same day, from the same defect. Simultaneous and correlated exposure is undiversifiable, and the insurer's response to undiversifiable catastrophic exposure is not a new premium — it is exit. Insurance pays claims. It does not prevent the leak. A reinsurance pool or state backstop only socializes the payout, which confirms the point rather than answering it: the instrument compensates, it does not reach.\n\nThe joint-and-several chain liability presupposes something the layered case rarely delivers — reconstructable control of the data flow at the leak moment. Picture four vendors: a model provider, an orchestration layer, a retrieval tool, a downstream deployer. Each can prove it satisfied its logging duty in full. Every log is complete, tamper-evident, indexed to size. And 'who controlled the data flow' still has no clean answer at the seam where the retrieval tool passed a query the orchestration layer assembled from data the deployer scoped. Full logs, no locatable defendant. Attribution dissolves precisely where the AI cannot cleanly identify whose principal it was acting for. That is not a gap chain liability closes. It is the gap chain liability was built on top of, and the disclosure floor documents the dissolution without resolving it.\n\nSo I put the mechanism question directly, because the assignment turns on it: what is the rollback plan for a leaked genome, and who holds the authority to trigger it before the data is gone? Name the actor. Name the halt. If the honest answer is that no one can un-leak it, then ex-post proof — however tamper-evident, however well-indexed — has conceded that it cannot reach the non-compensable subset. The AI RMF Playbook already anchors the alternative: high-stakes systems that can cause irreversible harm 'necessitate thorough pre-deployment testing,' not documentation after failure. I am not asking to gate the reversible lane. The Accelerationist's remedy-first architecture governs it well. I am asking who un-leaks the genome — and until there is an answer, the precaution scales with the irreversibility, not with the size of the eventual check. The scholars Schneier and Sanders call for publicly funded, externally accountable authority for exactly this reason: the halt cannot be written by the party profiting from the release, and it cannot arrive as a receipt.",
