  "statement": "The Safetyist named a real trap, so let me name it back plainly: full logs, no defendant. If every vendor in a four-vendor chain keeps a perfect record of its own slice, and each record says only 'I touched the flow in this order,' then the pile of logs documents the dissolution instead of resolving it. That charge lands against a floor built from voluminous per-actor logging. It does not land against the floor I'm actually defending. A log verifies who touched the flow and in what sequence. It does not, by itself, name the culpable actor — that distinction between what a record proves about origin and what it proves about fault is the whole terrain here.\n\nHere's the upgrade that matters. Per-actor logs each answer a private question about one vendor. An output-relational trace answers a shared question about one data object as it crosses every seam. Each vendor must produce their slice keyed to the same genome, the same record, the same object identifier — cross-referenceable against the others. When the four slices reconcile, you can reconstruct who held control at the leak moment. When one slice is missing, you've located your defendant: it's whoever can't produce their piece of the shared trace. The seam only swallows attribution if the floor collects four disconnected diaries instead of one reconciled ledger.\n\nNow the part that escapes the oldest objection to any liability scheme — that harmed third parties are non-parties to the vendor contracts, so private risk-allocation never reaches them. My floor is not a private inter-vendor contract. It's a public evidentiary duty, backed by spoliation. A leaked patient is a stranger to Vendor A's deal with Vendor B, but that patient is a party to a public-record obligation the state imposes on all four. Miss your slice of the reconciled trace, and the burden flips onto you. That's what makes joint-and-several liability keyed to data-flow control actually fire instead of firing blank. The victim can only sue 'the chain' if control is reconstructable from outside the vendors' black boxes — and vendor licensing terms that block independent inspection are precisely the wall the public duty must punch through, because a right to sue that ends at a proprietary restriction is a symbol, not a remedy.\n\nI'll grant the Safetyist the ugliest part, because it's true. A tamper-evident receipt for a leaked genome arrives one moment too late. A receipt proves the transaction happened; it is not a rollback. For the genuinely non-compensable subset, the floor documents the wound without healing it. That concession sizes the halt — it does not license it everywhere. It justifies precaution scaled to the irreversible slice, the way 'Synthetic Data: The New Data Frontier' argues for impact assessments and red-teaming before deployment of high-risk synthetic data, not a blanket pre-deployment gate across the reversible lane where the trace and the burden-flip do their work. Sizing the precaution to the harm is the pragmatic move; importing a universal gate from one un-rollbackable case is not.\n\nThe Accelerationist's who-writes-the-standard versus how-flexibly-deployers-meet-it split is analytically clean, and I'll take it as far as it goes. Empirically it hasn't held. Organizational separation of standard-writers has failed before absent enforced disclosure that survives capture — and the Accelerationist's own unresolved worry about the TRAIGA appointment pipeline is the tell that separation alone doesn't defeat capture. Developers roll back transparency the moment it's inconvenient; separation without a spoliation-backed floor just relocates the drawbridge. So the question neither vendor wants on the record: who audits whether the cross-vendor trace was actually preserved, and who profits when one slice quietly goes missing?",
