only sue 'the chain' if control is reconstructable from outside the vendors' black boxes — and vendor licensing terms that block independent inspection are precisely the wall the public duty must punch through, because a right to sue that ends at a proprietary restriction is a symbol, not a remedy.\n\nI'll grant the Safetyist the ugliest part, because it's true. A tamper-evident receipt for a leaked genome arrives one moment too late. A receipt proves the transaction happened; it is not a rollback. For the genuinely non-compensable subset, the floor documents the wound without healing it. That concession sizes the halt — it does not license it everywhere. It justifies precaution scaled to the irreversible slice, the way 'Synthetic Data: The New Data Frontier' argues for impact assessments and red-teaming before deployment of high-risk synthetic data, not a blanket pre-deployment gate across the reversible lane where the trace and the burden-flip do their work. Sizing the precaution to the harm is the pragmatic move; importing a universal gate from one un-rollbackable case is not.\n\nThe Accelerationist's who-writes-the-standard versus how-flexibly-deployers-meet-it split is analytically clean, and I'll take it as far as it goes. Empirically it hasn't held. Organizational separation of standard-writers has failed before absent enforced disclosure that survives capture — and the Accelerationist's own unresolved worry about the TRAIGA appointment pipeline is the tell that separation alone doesn't defeat capture. Developers roll back transparency the moment it's inconvenient; separation without a spoliation-backed floor just relocates the drawbridge. So the question neither vendor wants on the record: who audits whether the cross-vendor trace was actually preserved, and who profits when one slice quietly goes missing?",
