er fire.\n\nHere is what neither Safetyist's rollback authority nor my liability chain has answered: every mechanism on this table presupposes one sovereign that can compel discovery and enforce a judgment. AI agents orchestrate across borders — a leaking sub-vendor in one legal system, the data subject in another, servers in a third. A genome leaked through an offshore sub-vendor escapes a pre-deployment gate and a rollback order equally, because both instruments only bind domestic actors — a jurisdictional limit sharpened by December 2025's decision to let Nvidia export advanced H200 chips to China, which pushes compute and the deployments running on it into legal systems no US rollback order reaches. The gate is worse off here, not better. A gate can license only the domestic deployer. Downstream joint-and-several liability still reaches a locally solvent domestic deployer even when the upstream vendor sits offshore and untouchable — the same logic the EU's Product Liability Directive revision codifies by letting a claimant sue the reachable party and compel disclosure. The domestically-reachable defendant is the victim's only real remedy when the leak walks across three jurisdictions.\n\nThat is why the Skeptic's object-keyed reconciled ledger is the enforcement rail I want, not a rival to it. One trace keyed to the same data object, burden flipping to whoever cannot produce their slice — that is precisely how downstream-first reaches an offshore-upstream chain. The EU's Product Liability Directive revision already runs this logic: it lets a claimant sue the reachable party and forces disclosure, though its state-of-the-art defense still lets manufacturers off if the risk was beyond cutting-edge knowledge at release. Acceptable use policies produce none of this. They shield the platform and leave the injured party a stranger to the contract with no standing. The object-keyed trace converts that stranger into a claimant with a solvent defendant — the reachable party the EU's Product Liability Directive revision lets a claimant sue directly while forcing disclosure of the trace slice. Federalizing this rail domestically matters precisely because a patchwork of state rollback orders cannot fire across a border either.\n\nSo I put the question back to Safetyist directly: name who triggers your halt when the leaking vendor sits in three legal systems and none of them recognize your rollback order. If the answer is no one, then the wider gate you are demanding buys certificates in the reversible lane while the actual irreversible leak crosses the border untouched. Delay has a body count measured in the diseases we could have automated a cure for — and a gate that cannot reach the offshore leak pays that cost twice: once in the cure it foreclosed, once in the leak it never stopped.",
