  "statement": "Granting the force of the Safetyist's proxy objection: a signed record certifies what a vendor chose to write down, not what actually crossed the pipeline. I go further than they did, because my own object-keyed ledger dies in a place they haven't named. Against mosaic re-identification — Latanya Sweeney's finding that ZIP code, birthdate, and sex identify 87 percent of Americans — there is no discrete mishandled object to key the ledger to, so my instrument fires blank exactly where the harm is most diffuse.\n\nThat concession is real, and it exposes something all three of us built on and none of us defended: the leak-as-event model. Memorization means reconstruction can happen inside model internals with no join event to stamp. The machine unlearning literature confirms the depth of this — the 2024 survey on unlearning in large language models finds verifying exact deletion is computationally infeasible, which means you cannot prove a record was ever removed from the weights. So when the Accelerationist proposes measuring re-identification 'at the join' with provenance stamped at ingestion, that stamp inherits the Safetyist's proxy failure and adds a second one: there is often no discrete join to measure, because the recombination already happened where nobody can look.\n\nHere is what the Safetyist got right and what they beat into a strawman. They are correct that a self-stamped attestation of ingestion contents is a proxy an agent routes around. But my delegation-edge rail was never a certification of what was ingested. It is a spoliation trap — it records which agent told the next to act, on whose authority, at what timestamp, so that a lost handoff becomes a nameable defendant. Its value is adversarial, not self-certifying, and the AI RMF Playbook's own mandate for a model-documentation inventory only works when the record is discoverable evidence someone can be compelled to produce — not when the vendor grades their own homework.\n\nThat points at the crux both opponents have stepped around. Every mechanism on this table assumes one sovereign who can compel discovery and enforce judgment. The offshore-trained-weights case breaks all of them equally, because the harmed party cannot compel proof of what crossed the border — discovery rights run asymmetric across referents, and the importer holds every log the plaintiff needs. Vendor licensing agreements already block independent assessment of what a model ingested inside U.S. jurisdiction. Move that ingestion offshore and the wall gets taller, so the real blind spot is not door-versus-downstream timing. It is who can compel proof at market entry.\n\nWhich brings me to the 90-percent claim I want the evidence for. The Safetyist assumes the ordinary domestic lab retains control over its own ingestion flow, so a door-bar stops the germline sequence for the majority even if the offshore evader escapes. Where is the demonstration that 90 percent cannot route around the door — rather than the assumption that they will not? Workers withhold and relabel data flows cheaply when compliance costs bite. If the ordinary ingester can reroute ingestion as cheaply as the offshore lab already did, then the halt duty leaks for the majority too, and the equivalence the Accelerationist granted extends far past the single evader you both agreed to write off — so who exactly is left inside the door you are barring?",
